-- WHAT THIS PINS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT.
--
-- B-4b-ii supplied real interface identity at the CHECKER leg
-- (`findMatchingImplReqsU`).  The ROUTE-WORD leg, `entailInst`'s `EKNestedTop` arm,
-- selects one row on the interface its caller supplies and hands that row to
-- `argImplRequiresRoutesForRow`; on this top-level constrained-call leg that can be
-- the bare `csIface.irName` `pushDictApp` projects, which matches by spelling.
-- That asymmetry is the risk the bite takes on, and this fixture is where it is
-- observed rather than argued: the checker now selects `zmod.Same` by IDENTITY while
-- the router still selects by SPELLING over the same two candidate rows.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER, HAND-DERIVED FIRST: `useIt (Box Opaque)` instantiates `Same a` at
-- `Box Opaque` against `zmod.Same`, whose impl has no context, and its body calls
-- `foo` — 11. `bar (Box 1)` selects `amod`'s, `requires Eq a` grounds to `Eq Int` —
-- 22. Total 33. ACCEPT.
--
-- ⚠️ WHAT THE EMITTED IR ACTUALLY SHOWS, READ RATHER THAN ASSUMED. An earlier draft of
-- this header claimed the passed DICT was the assertion. It is not: `@mdk_zmod__useIt`
-- takes its dict in `%arg0` and then IGNORES it — its whole body is
-- `call i64 @mdk_impl_Box_foo(i64 %arg1)`, a DIRECT call the checker resolved
-- statically. So the route word is not consulted at run time on this shape, and a
-- claim resting on the dict constant would have been pinning a value nothing reads.
--
-- The assertion that IS discriminating is that direct call's TARGET: `useIt` reaches
-- `zmod`'s `foo`, and a router that had selected `amod`'s row would have named `bar`
-- there. Section 3 pins it, because a value-only row cannot tell a correct selection
-- from an arithmetic coincidence.
--
-- MEASURED ON THE B-4b-i BASE BINARY: rejected at exit 1 (`No impl of Eq for
-- Opaque`), the same false reject the `…-decl-order` sibling isolates — so this
-- program was UNREACHABLE before the supply, and the route leg had never been asked
-- this question. That is why it is pinned now rather than assumed.
import shared.{Box(..), Opaque(..)}
import amod.{bar}
import zmod.{useIt}

main = println (useIt (Box Opaque) + bar (Box 1))
